INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Cell Biology','easy','mcq','Which stain is used to classify bacteria into Gram-positive and Gram-negative groups based on cell wall composition?','["A. Ziehl-Neelsen stain","B. Gram stain","C. India ink stain","D. Silver stain"]','B','The Gram stain uses crystal violet and iodine (primary complex), then alcohol decolorisation, then safranin counterstain. Gram-positive organisms retain crystal violet (purple) due to thick peptidoglycan; Gram-negative organisms lose it and appear pink/red.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Cell Biology','easy','mcq','What is the primary structural component of the bacterial cell wall that provides rigidity and shape?','["A. Teichoic acid","B. Lipopolysaccharide","C. Peptidoglycan","D. Porin protein"]','C','Peptidoglycan (murein) is a cross-linked polymer of N-acetylglucosamine and N-acetylmuramic acid that forms the rigid cell wall layer in bacteria. It is present in all bacteria except Mycoplasma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Cell Biology','easy','mcq','Which organism lacks a cell wall entirely, making it intrinsically resistant to beta-lactam antibiotics?','["A. Staphylococcus aureus","B. Mycoplasma pneumoniae","C. Streptococcus pneumoniae","D. Clostridium perfringens"]','B','Mycoplasma pneumoniae has no cell wall and therefore lacks peptidoglycan. Beta-lactam antibiotics target penicillin-binding proteins involved in peptidoglycan synthesis, so Mycoplasma is inherently resistant to this drug class.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Cell Biology','medium','mcq','Lipid A, the toxic component of lipopolysaccharide (LPS), is found in which bacterial structure?','["A. Gram-positive cell wall","B. Bacterial cytoplasm","C. Gram-negative outer membrane","D. Bacterial ribosome"]','C','LPS is a major component of the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria. Lipid A anchors LPS into the outer membrane and is responsible for the endotoxin effects including fever, hypotension, and disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Cell Biology','medium','mcq','Teichoic acids are structural components unique to which type of bacteria?','["A. Gram-negative bacteria","B. Acid-fast bacteria","C. Gram-positive bacteria","D. Mycoplasma species"]','C','Teichoic acids are polyol phosphate polymers found in the cell walls of Gram-positive bacteria. They help maintain cell wall integrity, contribute to surface charge, and serve as virulence factors by aiding adherence to host tissues.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Cell Biology','medium','mcq','A patient develops septic shock after a Gram-negative bacteraemia. The fever, hypotension and DIC are primarily caused by which bacterial component?','["A. Exotoxin released by the bacteria","B. Lipid A component of LPS (endotoxin)","C. Peptidoglycan fragments","D. Bacterial capsular polysaccharide"]','B','Endotoxin (LPS) is released when Gram-negative bacteria lyse. Lipid A binds TLR4 on macrophages, triggering massive release of TNF-alpha, IL-1, and IL-6, leading to fever, vasodilation, hypotension, and DIC (the systemic inflammatory response syndrome).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Cell Biology','medium','mcq','Bacterial ribosomes are 70S, composed of 30S and 50S subunits. Which antibiotic class binds the 30S subunit to inhibit protein synthesis?','["A. Macrolides","B. Aminoglycosides","C. Chloramphenicol","D. Linezolid"]','B','Aminoglycosides (e.g., gentamicin, tobramycin) bind the 30S ribosomal subunit, causing misreading of mRNA and inhibiting translocation. Macrolides and chloramphenicol bind the 50S subunit. This distinction from eukaryotic 80S ribosomes (40S + 60S) underlies antibiotic selectivity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Cell Biology','medium','mcq','During the Gram stain procedure, what is the purpose of the iodine (mordant) step?','["A. It decolourises Gram-negative organisms","B. It forms an insoluble complex with crystal violet to fix the stain","C. It stains Gram-negative organisms red","D. It kills all bacteria on the slide"]','B','Iodine acts as a mordant, forming a large crystal violet-iodine (CV-I) complex within the cell that is too large to be washed out easily. In Gram-positive bacteria with thick peptidoglycan, dehydration by alcohol traps this complex; in Gram-negative bacteria it is washed away.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Cell Biology','hard','mcq','Mycobacterium tuberculosis is not well visualised by the standard Gram stain. What characteristic of its cell wall accounts for this and for its acid-fastness?','["A. Complete absence of peptidoglycan in its cell wall","B. High mycolic acid content creating a waxy, lipid-rich cell wall","C. Presence of an outer membrane like Gram-negative organisms","D. Production of enzymes that degrade crystal violet"]','B','Mycobacteria have a unique cell wall rich in long-chain mycolic acids (C60-C90 fatty acids) that make the wall highly hydrophobic and waxy. This prevents crystal violet from penetrating well, but also resists decolourisation by acid-alcohol in the Ziehl-Neelsen stain (acid-fast). They stain bright red against a blue background.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Cell Biology','hard','mcq','Penicillin inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs). Which specific step in peptidoglycan synthesis is blocked?','["A. Transport of NAM-NAG subunits across the cytoplasmic membrane via bactoprenol","B. Cross-linking of peptide side chains (transpeptidation)","C. Synthesis of UDP-N-acetylmuramic acid in the cytoplasm","D. Formation of the glycan backbone by transglycosylation"]','B','Beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems) are structural analogues of the D-Ala-D-Ala terminus of the peptide side chain. They covalently bind and inhibit PBPs (transpeptidases), preventing the cross-linking of peptidoglycan strands. This weakens the cell wall and leads to osmotic lysis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed')
,('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Virulence & Pathogenesis','easy','mcq','Which virulence factor allows bacteria to adhere to host epithelial cells and is the initial step in establishing infection?','["A. Capsule","B. Pili (fimbriae)","C. Flagella","D. Exotoxins"]','B','Pili (fimbriae) are hair-like surface appendages that mediate specific adherence of bacteria to host cell receptors. For example, type 1 pili of E. coli bind mannose residues on uroepithelial cells, initiating urinary tract infection. Adherence is a prerequisite for colonisation and infection.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Virulence & Pathogenesis','easy','mcq','A patient with an indwelling central venous catheter develops bacteraemia with coagulase-negative Staphylococci. Which virulence factor is most responsible for this infection?','["A. Pili-mediated adherence to mucosal surfaces","B. Exotoxin production causing tissue necrosis","C. Biofilm formation on the catheter surface","D. Capsule preventing complement deposition"]','C','Staphylococcus epidermidis and other coagulase-negative staphylococci form biofilms on prosthetic devices. Biofilm is a complex community of bacteria embedded in a self-produced extracellular polysaccharide matrix, protecting them from antibiotics and immune defences. Treatment requires device removal.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Virulence & Pathogenesis','easy','mcq','The capsule of Streptococcus pneumoniae is a major virulence factor. What is its primary mechanism of immune evasion?','["A. It degrades antibodies by producing proteases","B. It inhibits phagocytosis by preventing opsonisation","C. It inactivates complement proteins C3b and C5a","D. It blocks antigen presentation by MHC class II"]','B','Polysaccharide capsules are antiphagocytic. They mask bacterial surface antigens and prevent C3b opsonin deposition, thereby inhibiting recognition by phagocyte Fc receptors. Encapsulated organisms (S. pneumoniae, H. influenzae, N. meningitidis) are particularly dangerous in asplenic patients who rely on opsonisation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Virulence & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Cholera toxin causes profuse watery diarrhoea by a specific intracellular mechanism. Which best describes this mechanism?','["A. Direct invasion of colonocytes causing cell death","B. ADP-ribosylation of Gs protein, constitutively activating adenylyl cyclase and increasing cAMP","C. Inhibition of Na+/K+ ATPase causing cellular oedema","D. Activation of phospholipase C and the IP3 second messenger pathway"]','B','Cholera toxin (CT) is an A-B toxin. The B subunit binds GM1 ganglioside on enterocytes; the A subunit ADP-ribosylates the Gs alpha subunit, locking it in the active (GTP-bound) state. This constitutively activates adenylyl cyclase, raising cAMP, which activates CFTR chloride channels, leading to massive secretion of Cl-, Na+, and water into the gut lumen.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Virulence & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','A patient presents with descending flaccid paralysis after eating home-canned food. Which toxin is responsible and what is its mechanism?','["A. Tetanus toxin — blocks inhibitory neurotransmitter release at spinal inhibitory interneurons","B. Botulinum toxin — cleaves SNARE proteins to block acetylcholine release at the NMJ","C. Staphylococcal enterotoxin — superantigen stimulation of T cells","D. Diphtheria toxin — ADP-ribosylation of EF-2 inhibiting protein synthesis"]','B','Clostridium botulinum produces botulinum toxin, which cleaves SNARE proteins (VAMP, SNAP-25, syntaxin) required for acetylcholine vesicle fusion at the neuromuscular junction, causing flaccid paralysis (descending). Tetanus toxin causes spastic paralysis by blocking glycine/GABA release at inhibitory interneurons (ascending, from wound).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Virulence & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Which of the following organisms is classified as a facultative intracellular pathogen, enabling it to survive within phagocytes?','["A. Staphylococcus aureus","B. Streptococcus pyogenes","C. Listeria monocytogenes","D. Clostridium difficile"]','C','Listeria monocytogenes escapes the phagosome using listeriolysin O and actin-based motility (actin rockets) to spread directly between cells, evading extracellular antibodies and complement. Other facultative intracellular organisms include Salmonella, Mycobacterium, Brucella, and Legionella.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Virulence & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Toxic Shock Syndrome Toxin-1 (TSST-1) produced by Staphylococcus aureus is classified as a superantigen. What is the mechanism by which superantigens cause disease?','["A. They directly lyse red blood cells by forming pores in cell membranes","B. They cross-link MHC class II with TCR Vbeta, causing massive non-specific T-cell activation","C. They irreversibly inhibit acetylcholinesterase at nerve endings","D. They activate the classical complement pathway through antibody-independent binding to C1q"]','B','Superantigens bypass normal antigen presentation by simultaneously binding MHC class II on APCs and the Vbeta region of T-cell receptors, non-specifically activating up to 20-30% of all T cells. This triggers a massive cytokine storm (IL-1, IL-2, TNF) causing fever, rash, hypotension, and multi-organ failure.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Virulence & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Flagella in bacteria primarily serve which function relevant to pathogenesis?','["A. Antiphagocytic protection by masking surface antigens","B. Motility enabling bacteria to move towards host tissues and away from hostile environments","C. Adherence to host epithelial cell receptors","D. Production of toxins that disrupt cell membranes"]','B','Bacterial flagella are whip-like appendages that rotate to propel bacteria. They enable chemotaxis (movement toward nutrients/host tissue) and are important in establishing infection by allowing bacteria to reach target tissues. Flagellar H antigens are also key in serotyping Gram-negative organisms (e.g., E. coli O157:H7).','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Virulence & Pathogenesis','hard','mcq','A-B toxins consist of two functional components. Which statement correctly describes the role of each component?','["A. A subunit binds host cell receptor; B subunit has enzymatic activity causing cell damage","B. B subunit (binding) attaches to host cell receptors; A subunit (active/enzymatic) enters and causes toxic effects","C. Both A and B subunits must enter the cell together to cause toxicity","D. The B subunit directly inhibits protein synthesis in the host cell"]','B','In A-B toxins, the B (binding) subunit has high affinity for specific host cell surface receptors, facilitating endocytosis. The A (active) subunit possesses enzymatic activity and is responsible for the toxic effect. Examples include diphtheria toxin (B binds HB-EGF; A ADP-ribosylates EF-2), cholera toxin, and anthrax toxin.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Bacterial Virulence & Pathogenesis','hard','mcq','Quellung reaction is used to identify encapsulated organisms. What is observed during this test?','["A. Organisms turn bright red due to acid-fast staining of the capsule","B. The capsule appears to swell when type-specific anticapsular antibody is added, seen under light microscopy","C. Organisms agglutinate and form visible clumps in the test tube","D. A zone of haemolysis appears around colonies on blood agar"]','B','In the Quellung (capsule swelling) reaction, type-specific antisera against capsular polysaccharides bind the capsule, making it optically distinct and appearing swollen or refractile under light microscopy. This is used to serotype organisms like S. pneumoniae (93+ serotypes), H. influenzae, and N. meningitidis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed')
,('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Viral Replication & Pathogenesis','easy','mcq','Which class of RNA viruses requires RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (RdRp) packaged within the virion itself in order to initiate replication?','["A. Positive-sense single-stranded RNA viruses","B. Negative-sense single-stranded RNA viruses","C. Double-stranded DNA viruses","D. Retroviruses"]','B','Negative-sense (-) ssRNA viruses (e.g., influenza, rabies, Ebola, paramyxoviruses) cannot be directly translated by host ribosomes. They must carry their own RNA-dependent RNA polymerase (RdRp) to first transcribe the negative strand into positive-sense mRNA. Positive-sense RNA viruses can be directly translated.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Viral Replication & Pathogenesis','easy','mcq','HIV is a retrovirus that converts its RNA genome into DNA. Which enzyme is essential for this step and is a target for antiretroviral therapy?','["A. RNA polymerase","B. Reverse transcriptase","C. Integrase","D. Protease"]','B','Retroviruses (HIV-1, HIV-2, HTLV) carry reverse transcriptase (an RNA-dependent DNA polymerase) that converts the viral RNA genome into double-stranded DNA. This DNA is then integrated into the host chromosome by integrase. Nucleoside/non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors (NRTIs/NNRTIs) are key antiretroviral drug classes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Viral Replication & Pathogenesis','easy','mcq','Which mnemonic correctly identifies the major DNA viruses?','["A. HHAPPPY — Herpes, Hepadna, Adeno, Pox, Papilloma, Parvo, polyoma (Y)","B. DNAPOX — DNA, Adeno, Pox, Ortho, Xeno","C. ABCDE — Adeno, BK, CMV, Dengue, EBV","D. TORCH — Toxoplasma, Other viruses, Rubella, CMV, Herpes"]','A','The mnemonic HHAPPPy (or HHAPPP) helps recall DNA viruses: Herpesviruses, Hepadnaviruses (HBV), Adenovirus, Poxvirus, Papillomaviruses, Parvoviruses, and Polyomaviruses. Parvovirus is the exception — it is single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) while all others are double-stranded.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Viral Replication & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','During influenza outbreaks, antigenic shift results in pandemic strains. Which mechanism underlies antigenic shift?','["A. Gradual point mutations accumulating in haemagglutinin and neuraminidase genes","B. Reassortment of gene segments between two different influenza A strains infecting the same cell","C. Integration of influenza DNA into the host genome creating new epitopes","D. Recombination between influenza RNA genome and host cell mRNA"]','B','Antigenic shift involves reassortment of the 8 segmented RNA genome segments of influenza A when two different strains co-infect the same cell (typically in pigs or birds). This creates a novel HA/NA combination to which populations have no immunity, leading to pandemics (1918, 1957, 1968, 2009). Antigenic drift (minor mutations) causes seasonal epidemics.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Viral Replication & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Haemagglutinin (HA) and neuraminidase (NA) are surface glycoproteins of influenza. What are their respective functions?','["A. HA promotes viral budding; NA attaches the virus to host cells","B. HA attaches to sialic acid on host cells enabling entry; NA cleaves sialic acid enabling progeny virus release","C. HA evades host immune responses; NA catalyses RNA replication","D. Both HA and NA are involved only in antigenic variation and have no functional role in replication"]','B','Haemagglutinin binds to sialic acid (N-acetylneuraminic acid) receptors on host respiratory epithelial cells, mediating attachment and fusion. Neuraminidase cleaves sialic acid from host cells and newly formed virions, allowing progeny virus to be released. Oseltamivir (Tamiflu) inhibits neuraminidase.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Viral Replication & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','After primary infection, herpes simplex virus (HSV) establishes latency in which location?','["A. Liver hepatocytes","B. Sensory (dorsal root) ganglia neurons","C. Circulating B lymphocytes","D. Bone marrow stem cells"]','B','After primary HSV infection at mucosal surfaces, the virus travels retrograde along sensory nerves to establish latency in sensory ganglia (trigeminal ganglion for HSV-1; sacral ganglia for HSV-2). Reactivation by stress, UV light, or immunosuppression causes virus to travel anterograde back to the skin/mucosa causing recurrent lesions.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Viral Replication & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Parvovirus B19 is unique among DNA viruses. What feature distinguishes it from other DNA viruses?','["A. It replicates in the cytoplasm rather than the nucleus","B. It is a single-stranded DNA (ssDNA) virus, unlike other DNA viruses which are double-stranded","C. It uses reverse transcriptase to integrate into the host genome","D. It has a segmented genome like influenza"]','B','Parvovirus B19 is the only human pathogenic ssDNA virus (and is among the smallest viruses). All other human DNA viruses (HHAPPPy) are dsDNA. It causes erythema infectiosum (fifth disease) in children and aplastic crisis in sickle cell disease patients by infecting and destroying erythroid progenitors.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Viral Replication & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Which step in the viral replication cycle involves the virus inserting its genome into the host cell?','["A. Adsorption (attachment)","B. Penetration and uncoating","C. Biosynthesis","D. Maturation and release"]','B','After adsorption (attachment) of viral surface proteins to host cell receptors, penetration occurs via endocytosis or membrane fusion. Uncoating releases the viral genome from its protein coat into the cytoplasm or nucleus. Biosynthesis then produces new viral components, followed by assembly, maturation, and release.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Viral Replication & Pathogenesis','hard','mcq','Viral oncogenesis can occur through several mechanisms. Which best describes how high-risk HPV types (16 and 18) cause cervical cancer?','["A. Viral DNA integrates and expresses E6/E7 oncoproteins that degrade p53 and Rb tumour suppressors respectively","B. HPV reverse transcriptase introduces activating mutations into cellular proto-oncogenes","C. HPV capsid proteins directly bind and activate the EGF receptor constitutively","D. HPV induces production of telomerase by methylation of the hTERT gene promoter"]','A','High-risk HPV integrates into the host genome. HPV E6 oncoprotein binds and promotes proteasomal degradation of p53 (preventing apoptosis). HPV E7 binds and inactivates the retinoblastoma protein (Rb), releasing E2F transcription factors and driving uncontrolled cell proliferation. This is the key mechanism of HPV-associated cancers.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Viral Replication & Pathogenesis','hard','mcq','A positive-sense ssRNA virus differs from a negative-sense ssRNA virus in which critical way relevant to replication strategy?','["A. Positive-sense RNA can be directly translated by host ribosomes as mRNA; negative-sense RNA cannot and requires RdRp carried in the virion","B. Positive-sense RNA viruses always replicate in the nucleus; negative-sense RNA viruses replicate in the cytoplasm","C. Positive-sense RNA requires reverse transcriptase for replication; negative-sense RNA does not","D. Positive-sense RNA viruses always have an envelope; negative-sense RNA viruses are always non-enveloped"]','A','Positive-sense (+) ssRNA (e.g., hepatitis C, poliovirus, dengue, coronaviruses) has the same polarity as mRNA and can be directly translated by host ribosomes. Negative-sense (-) ssRNA (influenza, RSV, Ebola, rabies) is complementary to mRNA and must first be transcribed by a virion-carried RdRp before translation can occur.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed')
,('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Fungal Biology & Pathogenesis','easy','mcq','Most pathogenic fungi are dimorphic, existing in two forms depending on temperature. What is the correct rule for temperature-dependent dimorphism?','["A. Yeast form at 25°C (environmental mould); mould form at 37°C (body temperature)","B. Mould form at 25°C (environmental); yeast form at 37°C (body temperature)","C. Fungi are always in yeast form regardless of temperature","D. The switch from mould to yeast depends on pH, not temperature"]','B','The mnemonic for dimorphic fungi is ''Mould in the Cold, Yeast in the Heat'' (or ''37 = yeast''). At environmental temperatures (~25°C) they grow as moulds (hyphae); at body temperature (37°C) they convert to yeast (or spherules in Coccidioides). Key dimorphic pathogens: Histoplasma, Blastomyces, Coccidioides, Sporothrix, Paracoccidioides.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Fungal Biology & Pathogenesis','easy','mcq','Which stain is used to visualise the polysaccharide capsule of Cryptococcus neoformans in cerebrospinal fluid?','["A. Gram stain","B. Ziehl-Neelsen stain","C. India ink (nigrosin) stain","D. Periodic acid-Schiff (PAS) stain"]','C','India ink (or nigrosin) creates a dark background against which the clear halo of the Cryptococcus capsule is visible as a ''halo effect''. Cryptococcus neoformans causes meningitis in immunocompromised patients (AIDS with CD4 < 100). The capsule inhibits phagocytosis and is the major virulence factor.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Fungal Biology & Pathogenesis','easy','mcq','Amphotericin B is a polyene antifungal. What is its primary mechanism of action?','["A. Inhibits ergosterol synthesis by blocking 14-alpha-demethylase","B. Binds ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane, forming pores and causing cell lysis","C. Inhibits beta-1,3-glucan synthase, weakening the fungal cell wall","D. Inhibits fungal DNA gyrase, preventing DNA replication"]','B','Amphotericin B binds ergosterol (the major sterol in fungal cell membranes, analogous to cholesterol in human cells) and inserts into the membrane, creating transmembrane pores. This increases membrane permeability, causing leakage of intracellular ions and cell death. Its affinity for ergosterol over cholesterol gives selectivity, but nephrotoxicity is a major side effect.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Fungal Biology & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Histoplasma capsulatum and Blastomyces dermatitidis are endemic to a similar geographic region. Which region is most associated with these organisms?','["A. Southwestern United States desert (Arizona, New Mexico)","B. Mississippi and Ohio River valleys of the United States","C. Pacific Northwest and coastal regions of North America","D. Amazon basin of South America"]','B','Histoplasma capsulatum and Blastomyces dermatitidis are classically endemic to the Mississippi and Ohio River valleys. Histoplasma is associated with bat/bird droppings (spelunkers, construction workers). Blastomyces is found in moist soil near rivers. Coccidioides is endemic to the arid Southwest (Arizona, California).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Fungal Biology & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Echinocandin antifungals (e.g., caspofungin, micafungin) have a unique mechanism of action. What is their target?','["A. Beta-1,3-glucan synthase, disrupting fungal cell wall synthesis","B. Ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane","C. Lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), blocking ergosterol synthesis","D. Fungal ribosomes, inhibiting protein synthesis"]','A','Echinocandins inhibit beta-1,3-glucan synthase, an enzyme unique to fungi that polymerises beta-glucan into the fungal cell wall. Since mammalian cells lack cell walls, echinocandins have excellent tolerability. They are active against Candida and Aspergillus and are first-line for invasive candidiasis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Fungal Biology & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Azole antifungals (fluconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole) inhibit which enzyme involved in fungal ergosterol biosynthesis?','["A. Squalene epoxidase","B. Beta-glucan synthase","C. Lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP450 enzyme)","D. Thymidylate synthase"]','C','Azoles inhibit the fungal cytochrome P450 enzyme lanosterol 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51), blocking conversion of lanosterol to ergosterol. Depletion of ergosterol and accumulation of toxic sterol intermediates disrupt membrane integrity and function. Because azoles target CYP450, they have numerous drug interactions with medications metabolised by CYP enzymes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Fungal Biology & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Flucytosine (5-FC) is an antifungal with antimetabolite activity. What is its intracellular mechanism?','["A. Converted intracellularly to 5-fluorouracil (5-FU), inhibiting thymidylate synthase and RNA synthesis","B. It inhibits ergosterol synthesis by blocking CYP51","C. It binds ergosterol to form membrane pores","D. It inhibits the fungal proteasome, causing accumulation of misfolded proteins"]','A','Flucytosine is taken up by fungi via cytosine permease and converted to 5-fluorouracil (5-FU) by cytosine deaminase. 5-FU is further metabolised to 5-FUTP (inhibiting RNA synthesis) and 5-FdUMP (inhibiting thymidylate synthase, blocking DNA synthesis). It is used in combination with amphotericin B for cryptococcal meningitis to reduce emergence of resistance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Fungal Biology & Pathogenesis','medium','mcq','Coccidioides immitis is found in the southwestern United States. What is the pathognomonic tissue form seen in infected lungs?','["A. Broad-based budding yeast cells","B. Pseudohyphae with blastoconidia","C. Large spherules filled with endospores","D. Narrow-based budding yeast with a large capsule"]','C','Coccidioides immitis converts from arthroconidia (environmental form) to large spherules (10-80 micrometres) in host tissue. Each spherule fills with hundreds of endospores that are released when the spherule ruptures, spreading the infection. This is highly characteristic and differentiates Coccidioides from other dimorphic fungi.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Fungal Biology & Pathogenesis','hard','mcq','A major concern with amphotericin B therapy is nephrotoxicity. What is the primary mechanism by which amphotericin B causes renal damage?','["A. Direct vasoconstriction of afferent arterioles reducing GFR, plus direct tubular toxicity causing distal RTA","B. Immune complex deposition in the glomerular basement membrane","C. Inhibition of angiotensin-converting enzyme reducing filtration pressure","D. Crystallisation in tubular lumens causing obstructive nephropathy"]','A','Amphotericin B causes renal vasoconstriction (afferent arterioles), reducing GFR, and directly damages tubular epithelium. This results in azotaemia, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, and distal renal tubular acidosis. Liposomal amphotericin B was developed to reduce nephrotoxicity while maintaining antifungal efficacy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Fungal Biology & Pathogenesis','hard','mcq','Ergosterol is the primary sterol in fungal membranes. Why is ergosterol an ideal antifungal drug target compared to cholesterol in human cells?','["A. Ergosterol is structurally distinct from cholesterol, allowing selective drug binding to fungal membranes with less toxicity to human cells","B. Ergosterol is synthesised only in the nucleus, while cholesterol is made in the cytoplasm","C. Human cells do not use sterols, making ergosterol unique to fungi","D. Ergosterol is only found in yeast forms, not mould forms, restricting toxicity"]','A','Ergosterol differs from human cholesterol in its side chain and double bond configuration. This structural difference allows amphotericin B to bind ergosterol with much higher affinity than cholesterol, and allows azoles to target the fungal CYP51 enzyme selectively. However, some cross-reactivity with human cholesterol explains amphotericin B''s nephrotoxicity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed')
,('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Parasitology','easy','mcq','Which mosquito genus is the vector for Plasmodium species causing human malaria?','["A. Culex mosquito","B. Aedes mosquito","C. Anopheles mosquito","D. Mansonia mosquito"]','C','Plasmodium is transmitted exclusively by the female Anopheles mosquito. Aedes mosquitoes transmit dengue, Zika, yellow fever, and chikungunya. Culex transmits West Nile virus and lymphatic filariasis (Wuchereria bancrofti). Only female mosquitoes take blood meals (required for egg development), and so only females transmit disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Parasitology','easy','mcq','A patient from sub-Saharan Africa presents with cyclical fever every 48 hours. Which Plasmodium species is most consistent with this pattern?','["A. Plasmodium malariae","B. Plasmodium falciparum","C. Plasmodium vivax or Plasmodium ovale","D. Plasmodium knowlesi"]','C','Fever periodicity in malaria corresponds to erythrocyte lysis cycles. Plasmodium vivax and P. ovale cause tertian malaria with fever every 48 hours. P. malariae causes quartan malaria with fever every 72 hours (96-hour cycle). P. falciparum is irregular or can be daily, often masking the pattern.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Parasitology','easy','mcq','Toxoplasma gondii classically presents with ring-enhancing lesions on brain MRI in which patient population?','["A. HIV/AIDS patients with CD4 count below 100 cells/microlitre","B. Pregnant women in the second trimester","C. Patients on long-term corticosteroids after organ transplant","D. Neonates born to seropositive mothers"]','A','Toxoplasma gondii causes cerebral toxoplasmosis in severely immunocompromised patients (CD4 < 100). CNS MRI shows multiple ring-enhancing lesions, often in the basal ganglia. The diagnostic approach is empirical treatment with sulfadiazine + pyrimethamine; failure to respond suggests CNS lymphoma (the main differential).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Parasitology','medium','mcq','Plasmodium vivax and P. ovale can cause relapses months to years after initial infection. What is the mechanism?','["A. Hypnozoites (dormant liver-stage parasites) that can reactivate later","B. Persistent blood-stage parasites surviving in the spleen","C. Sequestration of parasites in deep vascular beds avoiding treatment","D. Re-infection from the same mosquito bite via delayed sporozoite release"]','A','P. vivax and P. ovale form hypnozoites — dormant liver stage parasites that can persist for months to years. Upon reactivation, they re-enter the erythrocytic cycle causing relapse. Primaquine (targeting liver stages) is required to achieve radical cure and prevent relapses. P. falciparum and P. malariae do not form hypnozoites.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Parasitology','medium','mcq','Plasmodium falciparum causes the most severe form of malaria. Which mechanism leads to cerebral malaria?','["A. Cytoadherence of infected erythrocytes to brain capillary endothelium via PfEMP1, causing microvascular obstruction","B. Direct invasion of neurones by merozoites released from infected red blood cells","C. Toxin-mediated vasoconstriction of cerebral arteries causing infarction","D. Immune complex deposition in cerebral vessels triggering an inflammatory cascade"]','A','P. falciparum-infected red blood cells express PfEMP1 (P. falciparum erythrocyte membrane protein 1) on their surface, which mediates cytoadherence to ICAM-1 and other receptors on capillary endothelium. This causes sequestration of infected RBCs in cerebral microvasculature, leading to obstruction, hypoxia, and cerebral malaria with high mortality.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Parasitology','medium','mcq','Schistosomiasis has a complex life cycle involving a specific intermediate host. Which organism serves as the intermediate host?','["A. Freshwater snail (genus Biomphalaria or Bulinus)","B. Freshwater fish","C. Anopheles mosquito","D. Domestic pig"]','A','Schistosoma species have a two-host life cycle. Eggs hatch in freshwater and miracidia infect freshwater snails (Biomphalaria for S. mansoni; Bulinus for S. haematobium). Free-swimming cercariae released by snails penetrate human skin during contact with contaminated water. Treatment is praziquantel.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Parasitology','medium','mcq','Onchocerca volvulus causes river blindness. Which vector transmits this filarial nematode?','["A. Anopheles mosquito","B. Culex mosquito","C. Simulium (blackfly)","D. Phlebotomus sandfly"]','C','Onchocerca volvulus is transmitted by Simulium (blackfly) species that breed near fast-flowing rivers (hence ''river blindness''). Microfilariae released by adult worms migrate to skin and eyes, where dying microfilariae trigger inflammatory reactions causing severe itching (onchodermatitis) and progressive visual loss. Treatment is ivermectin.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Parasitology','medium','mcq','Giardia lamblia infection typically presents with which characteristic stool finding?','["A. Watery, foul-smelling, fatty (greasy) stools due to fat malabsorption","B. Bloody diarrhoea with mucus (dysentery)","C. Rice-water stools without blood or mucus","D. Normal-formed stools with visible parasites"]','A','Giardia lamblia trophozoites attach to the duodenal and jejunal mucosa using their ventral disc, causing villous atrophy and malabsorption, particularly of fats and fat-soluble vitamins. Classic presentation: fatty (greasy), foul-smelling stools, bloating, flatulence, and abdominal cramps without blood or fever. Diagnosis: stool microscopy or antigen ELISA. Treatment: metronidazole.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Parasitology','hard','mcq','Wuchereria bancrofti causes lymphatic filariasis. What pathological mechanism leads to elephantiasis?','["A. Accumulation of adult worms in lymphatic vessels causing chronic lymphatic obstruction and oedema","B. Immune complex deposition in lymphatics triggering granulomatous inflammation","C. Toxin production by microfilariae causing lymphatic wall necrosis","D. Secondary bacterial infections causing irreversible lymph node fibrosis"]','A','Adult Wuchereria bancrofti worms live in lymphatic vessels and lymph nodes, causing chronic lymphangitis and lymphadenitis. Over years, persistent inflammation leads to fibrosis, lymphatic obstruction, and massive lymphoedema (elephantiasis) of limbs and genitalia. Microfilariae show nocturnal periodicity in peripheral blood, coinciding with Culex mosquito feeding times.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Parasitology','hard','mcq','A traveller returns from West Africa with recurrent fevers, and blood film shows ring-form trophozoites in >5% of red blood cells with banana-shaped gametocytes. Which diagnosis and treatment is most appropriate?','["A. Plasmodium falciparum malaria; treat with artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT)","B. Plasmodium vivax malaria; treat with chloroquine plus primaquine","C. Plasmodium malariae malaria; treat with chloroquine alone","D. Plasmodium ovale malaria; treat with chloroquine plus primaquine for radical cure"]','A','P. falciparum is identified by high parasitaemia (>5%), multiple ring-form trophozoites per RBC, and crescent/banana-shaped gametocytes. It is the only species causing life-threatening malaria (cerebral malaria, severe anaemia, respiratory failure). Artemisinin-based combination therapy (ACT, e.g., artemether-lumefantrine) is first-line treatment due to widespread chloroquine resistance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed')
,('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Host Defence & Innate Immunity','easy','mcq','TLR4 (Toll-like receptor 4) is a pattern recognition receptor on macrophages. What pathogen-associated molecular pattern (PAMP) does TLR4 primarily recognise?','["A. Flagellin from bacterial flagella","B. Double-stranded RNA from viruses","C. Lipopolysaccharide (LPS) from Gram-negative bacteria","D. Unmethylated CpG DNA from bacteria"]','C','TLR4 on macrophages recognises lipopolysaccharide (LPS) from Gram-negative bacteria in association with CD14 and MD-2. This triggers NF-kappaB activation and production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6, IL-12). TLR4 activation is central to the pathogenesis of Gram-negative sepsis. TLR5 detects flagellin; TLR3 detects dsRNA; TLR9 detects CpG DNA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Host Defence & Innate Immunity','easy','mcq','C3b is a complement fragment that plays a critical role in host defence. What is its primary function?','["A. It acts as an opsonin, coating bacteria to enhance phagocytosis","B. It forms the membrane attack complex (MAC) to lyse bacteria","C. It acts as a chemotactic factor to recruit neutrophils","D. It activates mast cells to release histamine"]','A','C3b is generated by cleavage of C3 during complement activation. It covalently binds to bacterial surfaces (opsonisation). Phagocytes (neutrophils, macrophages) express complement receptors (CR1/CD35) that bind C3b, greatly enhancing phagocytosis. C5a is the main chemotactic complement fragment; C5b-9 forms the MAC; C3a and C5a are anaphylatoxins.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Host Defence & Innate Immunity','easy','mcq','The spleen plays a critical role in protection against certain bacterial infections. Which type of organisms are patients most susceptible to after splenectomy?','["A. Encapsulated bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, and Neisseria meningitidis","B. Intracellular bacteria such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis","C. Anaerobic bacteria such as Bacteroides fragilis","D. Gram-negative enteric bacteria such as E. coli and Klebsiella"]','A','The spleen filters blood and is the primary site for opsonin-independent phagocytosis of encapsulated bacteria. Without splenic macrophages, patients cannot clear encapsulated organisms that resist complement-mediated opsonisation. OPSI (overwhelming post-splenectomy infection) is most commonly caused by S. pneumoniae (>50% of cases), H. influenzae type b, and N. meningitidis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Host Defence & Innate Immunity','medium','mcq','Which antimicrobial mechanisms are used by neutrophils within the phagolysosome to kill ingested bacteria?','["A. Reactive oxygen species (H2O2, superoxide), defensins, lysozyme, and lactoferrin","B. Antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity (ADCC) and NK cell activation","C. Interferon-gamma release and macrophage activation","D. Perforin and granzyme B-mediated apoptosis of infected cells"]','A','After phagocytosis, neutrophil granules fuse with the phagosome to form the phagolysosome. Killing occurs through: (1) oxidative burst producing ROS (superoxide via NADPH oxidase, H2O2, HOCl via myeloperoxidase); (2) non-oxidative mechanisms: lysozyme (degrades peptidoglycan), defensins (membrane disruption), lactoferrin (iron chelation), and proteases. ADCC and perforin/granzyme are NK cell and cytotoxic T cell mechanisms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Host Defence & Innate Immunity','medium','mcq','Facultative intracellular bacteria survive inside phagocytes. What is the most common mechanism used to achieve this?','["A. Inhibition of phagosome-lysosome fusion preventing exposure to lysosomal enzymes","B. Production of catalase to neutralise reactive oxygen species","C. Secretion of a thick capsule inside the phagosome","D. Induction of apoptosis of the phagocyte before killing occurs"]','A','Organisms such as Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Salmonella, Legionella, and Brucella survive inside macrophages by preventing fusion of the phagosome with lysosomes (inhibiting phagolysosome formation). This is mediated by various mechanisms including arrest of phagosome maturation and modification of phagosome membrane composition. This survival strategy allows immune evasion and dissemination.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Host Defence & Innate Immunity','medium','mcq','TNF-alpha and IL-1 are key cytokines in the systemic inflammatory response to infection. Which clinical manifestations are they primarily responsible for?','["A. Fever (via prostaglandin E2), acute-phase protein production, and hypotension in septic shock","B. Production of secretory IgA at mucosal surfaces","C. Activation of CD8+ cytotoxic T cells to kill infected cells","D. Class switching of B cells from IgM to IgG production"]','A','TNF-alpha and IL-1 are pleiotropic pro-inflammatory cytokines. Together they cause: fever (stimulate PGE2 from hypothalamus), acute phase response (hepatic CRP, fibrinogen, complement production), endothelial activation (ICAM-1 upregulation), and at high concentrations, vasodilation and hypotension contributing to septic shock.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Host Defence & Innate Immunity','medium','mcq','Opsonisation significantly enhances phagocytosis. Which molecules act as opsonins by coating bacteria for recognition by phagocyte receptors?','["A. IgG antibodies (via Fc receptors) and C3b (via complement receptors)","B. IgM antibodies and the C5a complement fragment","C. IgA antibodies and interferon-gamma","D. IgE antibodies and histamine"]','A','The two major opsonins are IgG (binds Fc receptors — FcgammaRIII/CD16 on phagocytes) and C3b (binds complement receptor CR1/CD35). Together they dramatically increase the efficiency of phagocytosis. This is why asplenic patients (who cannot opsonise efficiently) and patients with hypogammaglobulinaemia are susceptible to encapsulated bacteria.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Host Defence & Innate Immunity','hard','mcq','Chronic granulomatous disease (CGD) results in recurrent infections with catalase-positive organisms. What is the underlying defect?','["A. Deficiency of NADPH oxidase, impairing the respiratory burst and production of reactive oxygen species","B. Absence of the mannose receptor on macrophages, preventing phagocytosis of fungi","C. Deficiency of complement C3, preventing opsonisation of bacteria","D. Absence of toll-like receptors, preventing pattern recognition of pathogens"]','A','CGD is caused by mutations in NADPH oxidase components (most commonly gp91phox, X-linked). Without the oxidative burst, phagocytes cannot kill catalase-positive organisms (S. aureus, Aspergillus, Klebsiella, Serratia, Pseudomonas, Nocardia — mnemonic SPONK) because catalase neutralises the H2O2 the organisms make themselves, and NADPH oxidase cannot supplement it.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Host Defence & Innate Immunity','hard','mcq','The membrane attack complex (MAC) is the terminal effector of complement activation. Which complement components assemble to form the MAC?','["A. C1q, C1r, and C1s assembled on the bacterial surface","B. C3b and C4b forming an opsonin coat on bacteria","C. C5b, C6, C7, C8, and multiple C9 molecules forming a pore in the membrane","D. C3a and C5a forming anaphylatoxins that recruit inflammatory cells"]','C','After C5 cleavage, C5b initiates MAC assembly. C5b binds C6, then C7, then C8, and finally 12-18 molecules of C9 polymerise to form a transmembrane channel (pore). This MAC inserts into the target membrane, causing osmotic lysis. MAC is particularly important in defence against Neisseria species — patients with C5-C9 deficiency have recurrent Neisseria infections.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Host Defence & Innate Immunity','hard','mcq','C5a is a complement fragment with important roles in inflammation. What are its primary functions?','["A. Chemotaxis of neutrophils to sites of infection, mast cell degranulation, and upregulation of phagocyte oxidative burst","B. Opsonisation of bacteria and activation of the classical complement pathway","C. Formation of the membrane attack complex to lyse Gram-negative bacteria","D. Inhibition of complement activation to prevent tissue damage"]','A','C5a (generated from C5 cleavage by C5-convertase) is a potent anaphylatoxin with multiple roles: (1) chemotaxis — attracts neutrophils and monocytes to sites of infection; (2) mast cell/basophil degranulation releasing histamine; (3) upregulates phagocyte activity and oxidative burst; (4) increases vascular permeability. Together with C3a, it amplifies inflammatory responses at sites of complement activation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed')
,('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Adaptive Immunity & Vaccines','easy','mcq','CD4+ T helper cells are essential coordinators of adaptive immunity. What is their primary function in the immune response to infection?','["A. Recognising antigen on MHC class II and providing help (cytokines) to activate B cells and macrophages","B. Direct killing of virus-infected cells by releasing perforin and granzyme","C. Phagocytosis and intracellular killing of bacteria","D. Producing antibodies that neutralise bacterial toxins"]','A','CD4+ T helper cells recognise peptide antigens presented on MHC class II molecules (found on APCs: dendritic cells, macrophages, B cells). Upon activation they produce cytokines that: (1) help B cells produce antibodies (CD40L-CD40 interaction + IL-4, IL-21); (2) activate macrophages to kill intracellular pathogens (IFN-gamma); (3) recruit and activate other immune cells.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Adaptive Immunity & Vaccines','easy','mcq','Live-attenuated vaccines differ from inactivated vaccines in several important ways. Which statement is correct?','["A. Live-attenuated vaccines are safer in immunocompromised patients because the organism cannot replicate","B. Inactivated vaccines typically produce stronger immune responses requiring fewer doses than live vaccines","C. Live-attenuated vaccines can revert to virulence and are generally contraindicated in severely immunocompromised patients","D. Inactivated vaccines stimulate both humoral and cell-mediated immunity more effectively than live vaccines"]','C','Live-attenuated vaccines (MMR, varicella, yellow fever, oral typhoid, BCG, oral polio) use weakened organisms that replicate in the host, mimicking natural infection and generating strong, long-lasting humoral and cell-mediated immunity. However, they carry risk of reversion to virulence and are contraindicated in severe immunosuppression and pregnancy.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Adaptive Immunity & Vaccines','easy','mcq','HIV primarily destroys which immune cell type, leading to susceptibility to opportunistic infections?','["A. CD8+ cytotoxic T lymphocytes","B. Natural killer (NK) cells","C. CD4+ T helper lymphocytes","D. Plasma B cells"]','C','HIV uses gp120 to bind CD4 receptors (with CCR5 or CXCR4 as co-receptors) on CD4+ T helper cells, dendritic cells, and macrophages. Progressive destruction of CD4+ T cells leads to immunodeficiency. When CD4 count falls below 200 cells/microlitre, AIDS is defined and patients become susceptible to opportunistic infections (PCP, toxoplasmosis, CMV, MAC).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Adaptive Immunity & Vaccines','medium','mcq','Toxoid vaccines are used to prevent diphtheria and tetanus. What principle underlies the protective immunity they confer?','["A. They stimulate cell-mediated immunity to kill Corynebacterium and Clostridium directly","B. They introduce live attenuated organisms that colonise and confer colonisation resistance","C. They generate antibodies against inactivated toxins; these antibodies neutralise the actual toxins produced during infection","D. They activate NK cells to destroy toxin-producing bacteria through ADCC"]','C','Toxoid vaccines contain chemically inactivated (formaldehyde-treated) bacterial exotoxins. They retain immunogenicity but lack toxicity. The immune response generates toxin-neutralising antibodies (antitoxins), which neutralise the toxin if the organism infects the immunised person. They do not prevent colonisation but prevent disease manifestations (diphtheria, tetanus).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Adaptive Immunity & Vaccines','medium','mcq','Conjugate vaccines against Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) and pneumococcus link capsular polysaccharide to a protein carrier. Why is this conjugation necessary?','["A. The protein carrier increases the size of the vaccine particle to prevent rapid renal clearance","B. The carrier protein directly stimulates innate immunity which is required before adaptive immunity can respond","C. Polysaccharides alone are T-independent antigens that generate poor memory responses in children under 2; conjugation to a protein converts them to T-dependent antigens with memory cell generation","D. Conjugation reduces toxicity of the capsular polysaccharide at injection sites"]','C','Pure polysaccharide antigens are T-independent antigens that activate B cells directly without T helper cell involvement, producing predominantly IgM without affinity maturation or memory. Children under 2 have immature T-independent responses. Conjugation to a protein (e.g., diphtheria toxoid, tetanus toxoid) creates a T-dependent antigen, generating robust IgG responses and immunological memory.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Adaptive Immunity & Vaccines','medium','mcq','Varicella-zoster virus (VZV) causes chickenpox on primary infection and reactivates to cause shingles. Where does VZV establish latency?','["A. Hepatocytes of the liver","B. Circulating CD4+ memory T lymphocytes","C. Dorsal root ganglia sensory neurons","D. Bone marrow B cell precursors"]','C','After primary VZV infection (chickenpox), the virus travels retrograde along sensory nerves and establishes latency in dorsal root ganglia (or trigeminal ganglia) neurons. Reactivation — triggered by immunosuppression, stress, or ageing — results in herpes zoster (shingles), characterised by vesicular rash in a dermatomal distribution, often with postherpetic neuralgia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Adaptive Immunity & Vaccines','medium','mcq','Cell-mediated immunity (CMI) is the primary defence against intracellular pathogens. Which pathogens are immunocompromised patients (CMI deficiency) most susceptible to?','["A. Extracellular bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae","B. Helminthic parasites requiring eosinophil-mediated killing","C. Intracellular organisms such as Mycobacteria, Listeria, Salmonella, fungi, viruses, and parasites (e.g., Toxoplasma)","D. Bacterial toxin-mediated diseases such as tetanus and diphtheria"]','C','CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and activated macrophages (via IFN-gamma from CD4+ Th1 cells) are essential for eliminating intracellular pathogens. Deficiency of CMI (HIV/AIDS, organ transplantation, lymphoma) predisposes to Mycobacterium tuberculosis/avium, Listeria, Salmonella, CMV, HSV, VZV, Candida, Cryptococcus, Aspergillus, Toxoplasma, Cryptosporidium, and PCP (Pneumocystis).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Adaptive Immunity & Vaccines','hard','mcq','Immunological memory allows rapid secondary immune responses. Which features distinguish a secondary (anamnestic) response from a primary immune response?','["A. The secondary response is slower, produces only IgM, and involves fewer cells","B. The secondary response requires re-exposure to the same APC that processed antigen in the primary response","C. The secondary response is faster, produces higher titres of class-switched IgG with higher affinity, and involves pre-existing memory B and T cells","D. The secondary response is identical to the primary response but occurs in peripheral blood rather than lymph nodes"]','C','During the primary response, naive B cells undergo activation, proliferation, somatic hypermutation (affinity maturation), and class switching, generating plasma cells and memory B cells. Upon re-exposure, memory B cells respond rapidly (lag phase 1-3 days vs 7-10 days), producing higher titres of high-affinity IgG. This principle underlies booster vaccinations and herd immunity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Adaptive Immunity & Vaccines','hard','mcq','Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) establishes latency in B lymphocytes. How does EBV cause infectious mononucleosis and what is the characteristic immune response?','["A. EBV directly infects T lymphocytes, causing their uncontrolled proliferation and liver infiltration","B. EBV infects dendritic cells, impairing antigen presentation and allowing secondary bacterial superinfection","C. EBV infects B cells via CD21 (CR2), causing polyclonal B cell activation; the characteristic atypical lymphocytes are reactive CD8+ T cells targeting EBV-infected B cells","D. EBV infects NK cells, disrupting innate immunity and allowing uncontrolled B cell viral replication"]','C','EBV binds CD21 (complement receptor 2, CR2) on B cells and HLA class II to infect them, causing polyclonal activation. Reactive CD8+ cytotoxic T cells (appearing as ''atypical lymphocytes'' on blood film) proliferate to control infected B cells. Classic features: pharyngitis, lymphadenopathy, splenomegaly, hepatitis, and positive monospot test (heterophile antibodies).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Adaptive Immunity & Vaccines','hard','mcq','Which vaccine type uses a killed whole pathogen and generally requires multiple doses and adjuvants to generate adequate immunity?','["A. Live-attenuated vaccine","B. Conjugate polysaccharide vaccine","C. Toxoid vaccine","D. Inactivated (killed) vaccine"]','D','Inactivated vaccines (e.g., inactivated polio vaccine/IPV, inactivated influenza, hepatitis A, rabies, whole-cell pertussis) use chemically or heat-killed organisms. They cannot replicate in the host, so they generate weaker immune responses and require adjuvants and multiple doses (primary series + boosters) to achieve protective immunity. They are safer in immunocompromised patients but less immunogenic than live vaccines.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed')
,('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Antimicrobial Resistance Mechanisms','easy','mcq','Beta-lactamase enzymes are the most common mechanism of resistance to penicillins and cephalosporins. Where are these enzymes located in Gram-negative bacteria versus Gram-positive bacteria?','["A. In the cytoplasm of both Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria","B. On the inner (cytoplasmic) membrane in all bacteria","C. On the outer membrane of Gram-negative bacteria; in the cell wall of Gram-positive bacteria","D. In the periplasmic space of Gram-negative bacteria; secreted extracellularly by Gram-positive bacteria"]','D','In Gram-negative bacteria, beta-lactamases are confined to the periplasmic space between the inner and outer membranes, where they can hydrolyse beta-lactams as they attempt to reach PBPs. Gram-positive bacteria (which lack an outer membrane) secrete beta-lactamases into the surrounding medium. This location affects the efficiency and kinetics of resistance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Antimicrobial Resistance Mechanisms','easy','mcq','MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) is resistant to all beta-lactam antibiotics. What is the mechanism of this resistance?','["A. MRSA produces a beta-lactamase that hydrolyses all beta-lactam antibiotics","B. MRSA uses efflux pumps to actively remove beta-lactam antibiotics from the periplasm","C. MRSA develops porin mutations preventing beta-lactam entry","D. MRSA acquires the mecA gene encoding PBP2A (PBP2''), an altered penicillin-binding protein with low affinity for all beta-lactams"]','D','MRSA carries the mecA gene (on mobile SCCmec elements) encoding PBP2A (PBP2''), an alternative penicillin-binding protein with extremely low affinity for all beta-lactam antibiotics. PBP2A can perform transpeptidation even when all other PBPs are inhibited, conferring resistance to penicillins, cephalosporins, and carbapenems. Treatment: vancomycin, linezolid, or daptomycin.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Antimicrobial Resistance Mechanisms','easy','mcq','Efflux pumps are a resistance mechanism that actively removes antibiotics from bacteria. Which antibiotic class is classically associated with efflux pump-mediated resistance?','["A. Beta-lactams","B. Aminoglycosides","C. Tetracyclines","D. Glycopeptides"]','C','Tetracycline resistance in many Gram-negative and Gram-positive bacteria is mediated by energy-dependent efflux pumps (e.g., TetA in E. coli) that export tetracycline from the bacterial cell before it can bind 30S ribosomes. Efflux pumps also contribute to resistance to fluoroquinolones and macrolides in some organisms. Aminoglycosides are inactivated by modifying enzymes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Antimicrobial Resistance Mechanisms','medium','mcq','Vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus (VRE) VanA type resistance occurs by which mechanism?','["A. Production of beta-lactamase that hydrolyses the glycopeptide ring of vancomycin","B. Mutation of penicillin-binding proteins to reduce affinity for vancomycin","C. Overexpression of efflux pumps that remove vancomycin from the periplasm","D. Substitution of D-Ala-D-Ala terminus of peptidoglycan precursors with D-Ala-D-Lactate, reducing vancomycin binding affinity"]','D','Vancomycin inhibits cell wall synthesis by binding the D-Ala-D-Ala terminus of peptidoglycan precursors, preventing transglycosylation and transpeptidation. VanA resistance (on transferable Tn1546 transposon) encodes enzymes that substitute D-Ala-D-Lactate for D-Ala-D-Ala. This single substitution reduces vancomycin binding affinity ~1000-fold while maintaining cell wall function.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Antimicrobial Resistance Mechanisms','medium','mcq','Extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBLs) are commonly produced by Klebsiella pneumoniae and E. coli. What is the clinical implication for antibiotic choice?','["A. ESBL producers are resistant only to aminopenicillins; cephalosporins remain effective","B. ESBL producers can be treated with beta-lactam/beta-lactamase inhibitor combinations such as ampicillin-sulbactam","C. ESBL producers are resistant to carbapenems but remain susceptible to third-generation cephalosporins","D. ESBL producers are resistant to all penicillins and cephalosporins; carbapenems are the treatment of choice"]','D','ESBLs are plasmid-encoded enzymes derived from TEM or SHV penicillinases by mutations extending their spectrum to include third-generation cephalosporins (ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, ceftazidime) and aztreonam. ESBLs are inhibited by clavulanate in vitro but standard combinations fail clinically. Carbapenems (meropenem, ertapenem) are the treatment of choice for serious ESBL infections.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Antimicrobial Resistance Mechanisms','medium','mcq','Porin channel loss is a mechanism of carbapenem resistance in Pseudomonas aeruginosa. How does this mechanism work?','["A. Loss of outer membrane porin OprD prevents imipenem from reaching its PBP target in the periplasm","B. Upregulation of beta-lactamase production increases enzymatic inactivation of carbapenems","C. PBP mutation reduces affinity for all carbapenems across all Pseudomonas strains","D. Biofilm formation prevents carbapenem penetration into deep tissue infections"]','A','Carbapenems enter Gram-negative bacteria through specific outer membrane porin channels (OprD in Pseudomonas). Loss of OprD through mutation or downregulation prevents imipenem and meropenem from reaching their PBP targets in the periplasm, conferring carbapenem resistance. This can be combined with upregulation of AmpC beta-lactamase and efflux pumps for high-level resistance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Antimicrobial Resistance Mechanisms','medium','mcq','Resistance genes are often transferred between bacteria by horizontal gene transfer. Which mobile genetic elements most commonly carry resistance genes between bacteria?','["A. Chromosomal mutations that arise spontaneously under antibiotic pressure","B. Outer membrane vesicles containing resistance enzymes","C. Bacteriophages carrying resistance genes in their structural proteins","D. Plasmids and transposons that can be transferred via conjugation, transformation, or transduction"]','D','Horizontal gene transfer (HGT) is the major driver of resistance spread. Plasmids (R-plasmids) carrying resistance genes are transferred between bacteria by conjugation (F pilus-mediated direct contact), transformation (uptake of naked DNA), or transduction (bacteriophage-mediated). Transposons (''jumping genes'') can move resistance genes between plasmids and chromosomes. This allows rapid spread across species.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Antimicrobial Resistance Mechanisms','hard','mcq','KPC (Klebsiella pneumoniae carbapenemase) and NDM-1 (New Delhi Metallo-beta-lactamase) are carbapenemase enzymes. Which antibiotic class, if any, remains reliably effective against KPC-producing Enterobacteriaceae?','["A. Third-generation cephalosporins such as ceftriaxone","B. Carbapenems at high doses remain effective","C. Colistin (polymyxin E), sometimes combined with other agents, is often the only remaining option","D. Vancomycin is effective against KPC-producing Klebsiella"]','C','KPC and NDM-1 hydrolyse all beta-lactams including carbapenems. NDM-1 additionally often co-exists with other resistance genes. The primary remaining therapeutic options are colistin/polymyxin B (targeting the outer membrane), tigecycline, and newer combinations (ceftazidime-avibactam for KPC, but not NDM-1). Vancomycin does not penetrate the outer membrane of Gram-negative organisms.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Antimicrobial Resistance Mechanisms','hard','mcq','Aminoglycoside resistance is mediated by modifying enzymes. Which types of enzymes are responsible and what do they do?','["A. Efflux pumps that actively export aminoglycosides before they reach the 30S ribosome","B. Beta-lactamases with extended spectrum activity that also degrade aminoglycoside ring structures","C. Methyltransferases that modify the 23S rRNA to prevent aminoglycoside binding to the 50S subunit","D. Aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes (acetyltransferases, phosphotransferases, adenylyltransferases) that chemically modify the drug, reducing 30S ribosome binding"]','D','Aminoglycoside-modifying enzymes (AMEs) are the primary resistance mechanism. Acetyltransferases (AACs), phosphotransferases (APHs), and adenylyltransferases (ANTs) chemically modify aminoglycosides at specific hydroxyl or amino groups. This prevents the drug from binding to its 30S ribosomal target with sufficient affinity. AME genes are commonly plasmid-encoded and transferable.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Antimicrobial Resistance Mechanisms','hard','mcq','A laboratory reports a Staphylococcus aureus isolate with inducible clindamycin resistance detected by the D-zone test. What mechanism underlies this resistance?','["A. mecA-encoded PBP2A with cross-resistance to clindamycin and all beta-lactams","B. Plasmid-encoded clindamycin acetyltransferase induced by erythromycin as a co-inducer","C. Active efflux of clindamycin via MefA pump inducible by erythromycin","D. Methylation of 23S rRNA by erm genes (MLSB resistance), inducibly expressed after erythromycin exposure, conferring cross-resistance to macrolides, lincosamides, and streptogramins"]','D','Erm (erythromycin ribosome methylation) genes encode methyltransferases that methylate adenine in 23S rRNA, preventing macrolide, lincosamide (clindamycin), and streptogramin B binding (MLSB resistance). In inducible strains, erm is expressed only after erythromycin exposure (detected by D-zone test). Clindamycin therapy can select for constitutive mutants, so it should be avoided despite apparent susceptibility.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed')
,('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Laboratory Diagnosis','easy','mcq','A Gram stain of a wound swab shows Gram-positive cocci in clusters. Which organism is most likely?','["A. Streptococcus pyogenes","B. Enterococcus faecalis","C. Neisseria gonorrhoeae","D. Staphylococcus aureus"]','D','Gram-positive cocci in clusters (''bunch of grapes'' arrangement) are characteristic of Staphylococcus species. S. aureus is coagulase-positive. S. epidermidis and other coagulase-negative staphylococci are common skin commensals and nosocomial pathogens. Streptococci appear in chains; Enterococci in pairs or short chains; Neisseria as Gram-negative diplococci.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Laboratory Diagnosis','easy','mcq','Which culture medium is selective for Neisseria gonorrhoeae, inhibiting the growth of most other organisms?','["A. MacConkey agar","B. Blood agar with 5% sheep blood","C. Sabouraud dextrose agar","D. Thayer-Martin agar"]','D','Thayer-Martin (modified) agar is chocolate agar (lysed blood providing hemin and NAD) supplemented with antibiotics (vancomycin, colistin, nystatin, trimethoprim) to inhibit Gram-positives, Gram-negatives, and fungi while allowing Neisseria and Haemophilus to grow. It is the selective medium for gonorrhoea culture from urogenital specimens.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Laboratory Diagnosis','easy','mcq','A patient is being tested for HIV infection. Which combination of tests is used for screening and then confirmation?','["A. PCR (viral load) for screening; CD4 count for confirmation","B. Rapid antigen test for screening; CD4 count below 200 cells/microlitre for confirmation","C. Western blot for screening; ELISA for confirmation","D. ELISA (detects HIV antibodies/p24 antigen) for screening; Western blot for confirmation"]','D','HIV testing strategy: (1) Fourth-generation ELISA (detects both HIV antibody and p24 antigen — reduces window period) is used for initial screening. A reactive ELISA is confirmed by (2) Western blot (or HIV-1/HIV-2 differentiation immunoassay), which detects antibodies to specific HIV proteins (gp120, gp41, p24). Both tests must be reactive for a confirmed diagnosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Laboratory Diagnosis','medium','mcq','A patient with AIDS and a CD4 count of 50 presents with fever and respiratory symptoms. BAL (bronchoalveolar lavage) shows cysts staining positive with silver (GMS) stain. What is the diagnosis?','["A. Aspergillus fumigatus infection","B. Histoplasma capsulatum infection","C. Cryptococcal pneumonia","D. Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)"]','D','Pneumocystis jirovecii (formerly P. carinii) cysts are best visualised with methenamine silver (GMS) stain or toluidine blue O, appearing as ''crushed ping-pong ball'' shaped cysts. It is the most common AIDS-defining opportunistic infection when CD4 < 200 cells/microlitre. Classic presentation: progressive dyspnoea, dry cough, low-grade fever, bilateral ground-glass infiltrates on CXR. Treatment: TMP-SMX.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Laboratory Diagnosis','medium','mcq','Bacterial meningitis and viral meningitis produce different CSF findings. Which CSF profile is consistent with bacterial meningitis?','["A. Clear fluid, lymphocytosis (100-1000 cells), normal glucose, mildly elevated protein","B. Clear fluid, eosinophilia, normal glucose, mildly elevated protein","C. Xanthochromic fluid, red blood cells, normal glucose, normal protein","D. Turbid fluid, neutrophilia (>1000 cells), low glucose (<2.2 mmol/L), markedly elevated protein (>1 g/L)"]','D','Bacterial meningitis CSF: turbid/purulent appearance; predominantly neutrophils (polymorphonuclear cells), often >1000/microlitre; low glucose (<2.2 mmol/L or CSF:serum ratio <0.4) due to bacterial consumption and impaired transport; elevated protein (>0.45 g/L). Viral meningitis: clear fluid, lymphocytic pleocytosis, normal or slightly low glucose, mildly elevated protein.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Laboratory Diagnosis','medium','mcq','MacConkey agar is used to isolate and differentiate Gram-negative enteric bacteria. What does a pink/red colony on MacConkey agar indicate?','["A. The organism is Gram-positive and produced haemolysis","B. The organism produces hydrogen sulphide, forming black-centred colonies","C. The organism is a non-fermenter producing alkali (e.g., Pseudomonas)","D. The organism is a lactose fermenter (e.g., E. coli, Klebsiella)"]','D','MacConkey agar contains lactose, bile salts (inhibit Gram-positives), and neutral red pH indicator. Lactose-fermenting organisms (E. coli, Klebsiella, Enterobacter) produce acid, turning neutral red to pink/red (''pink colonies''). Non-lactose fermenters (Salmonella, Shigella, Pseudomonas) appear colourless/pale. This initial differentiation guides further identification and helps detect potential pathogens.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Laboratory Diagnosis','medium','mcq','IgM and IgG serology are used to determine the timing of infection. Which pattern suggests acute primary infection versus past infection/immunity?','["A. Elevated IgG only suggests acute infection; elevated IgM only suggests past infection","B. IgG avidity testing is required first before IgM or IgG interpretation","C. Both IgM and IgG must be elevated to diagnose acute infection","D. Elevated IgM (or rising IgM titre) suggests acute/recent infection; elevated IgG alone (with negative IgM) suggests past infection or immunity"]','D','IgM is the first antibody produced in primary infection (appears within days to weeks) and typically declines within months. IgG appears slightly later, persists for years (often lifelong), and reflects past infection or immunity. Acute infection: IgM positive (or 4-fold rise in paired IgG titres 2-4 weeks apart). Past infection/immunity: IgM negative, IgG positive.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Laboratory Diagnosis','medium','mcq','PCR (polymerase chain reaction) is used for diagnosis of many infections. What are the key advantages of PCR over culture-based methods?','["A. PCR is cheaper and requires less technical expertise than culture","B. PCR requires less stringent sample handling and transport conditions than culture","C. PCR gives information about antibiotic susceptibility, unlike culture","D. PCR provides very high sensitivity and specificity, can detect non-viable organisms, gives rapid results, and can be used for slow-growing or non-culturable organisms"]','D','PCR amplifies specific nucleic acid sequences, allowing detection of as few as 1-10 copies of pathogen DNA/RNA. Advantages: high sensitivity (detects non-viable/dead organisms), high specificity (pathogen-specific primers), rapid results (hours vs days/weeks for culture), works for non-culturable organisms (e.g., Tropheryma whipplei) and viruses. It is used for HIV viral load, TB diagnosis (GeneXpert), and CSF meningitis/encephalitis panels.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Laboratory Diagnosis','hard','mcq','KOH (potassium hydroxide) preparation is used for direct microscopy of specimens. What does KOH do and what does it diagnose?','["A. KOH stains bacteria blue, allowing Gram-stain-like differentiation on fresh specimens","B. KOH lyses red blood cells, revealing intracellular parasites such as Plasmodium","C. KOH precipitates bacterial cell walls, making them visible without staining","D. KOH dissolves keratin and cellular debris, making fungal hyphae and elements visible against a cleared background"]','D','10-20% KOH dissolves keratin (in skin scrapings, hair, nails) and digests cellular material, but leaves fungal cell walls (chitin) intact. This allows direct visualisation of fungal hyphae, pseudohyphae, or spores under light microscopy without staining. KOH prep is used to diagnose dermatophytoses (tinea), candidiasis, and other superficial/subcutaneous fungal infections.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Laboratory Diagnosis','hard','mcq','A CSF Gram stain from a neonate shows Gram-positive rods. Which organism is most likely and what is the significance?','["A. Listeria monocytogenes — a facultative intracellular pathogen causing neonatal meningitis acquired transplacentally or during delivery","B. Clostridium perfringens — a spore-forming anaerobe causing gas gangrene of the CNS","C. Bacillus anthracis — acquired by inhalation of anthrax spores in a bioterrorism event","D. Corynebacterium diphtheriae — producing diphtheria toxin causing CNS demyelination"]','A','Listeria monocytogenes is the only Gram-positive rod causing meningitis in neonates, pregnant women, elderly, and immunocompromised individuals. Neonates acquire it transplacentally (early-onset, <7 days) or during birth (late-onset). It is intrinsically resistant to cephalosporins. Treatment of choice is ampicillin (+ gentamicin synergy). It grows at 4°C (''cold enrichment'') and shows tumbling motility.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed')
,('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Infection Control & Epidemiology','easy','mcq','Sterilisation by autoclave (steam under pressure, 121°C for 15-20 minutes) achieves which level of microbial killing?','["A. Kills all vegetative bacteria and viruses but not bacterial endospores","B. Equivalent to high-level disinfection, killing most but not all spores","C. Kills all bacteria but not prions or certain viruses","D. Kills all microorganisms including bacterial endospores (most heat-resistant life forms)"]','D','Autoclaving uses saturated steam under pressure (121°C at 15 psi for 15-20 minutes, or 134°C for 3-4 minutes) to achieve sterilisation — killing all microorganisms including the most resistant bacterial endospores (Bacillus, Clostridium). It is the gold standard for sterilising heat-stable instruments, media, and equipment. Prions require more extreme conditions (134°C for 18 minutes or chemical treatment).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Infection Control & Epidemiology','easy','mcq','Which single infection control measure is most effective at preventing the spread of healthcare-associated infections?','["A. Routine use of gloves for all patient contact","B. Routine environmental disinfection of ward surfaces with bleach","C. Universal gowning for all healthcare workers in clinical areas","D. Handwashing (hand hygiene) with soap/water or alcohol-based hand rub"]','D','Hand hygiene is consistently demonstrated to be the single most effective measure for preventing healthcare-associated infections. The WHO Five Moments for Hand Hygiene (before patient contact, before aseptic procedures, after body fluid exposure, after patient contact, after contact with patient surroundings) captures when hand decontamination is required. Alcohol hand rub kills most organisms rapidly.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Infection Control & Epidemiology','easy','mcq','Tuberculosis, measles, and varicella require airborne precautions. What constitutes airborne transmission?','["A. Direct contact with contaminated surfaces followed by touching the face","B. Large respiratory droplets (>5 micrometres) that settle within 1 metre","C. Tiny respiratory particles (<5 micrometres) that remain suspended in the air and can travel long distances","D. Transmission through contaminated food or water"]','C','Airborne transmission involves particles <5 micrometres (droplet nuclei) that remain suspended in air for extended periods and travel >1 metre. This requires: airborne isolation (negative pressure room), N95 respirator (FFP3 in UK) for healthcare workers. Organisms: M. tuberculosis, measles virus, varicella-zoster virus (chickenpox). Droplet transmission (>5 micrometres, <1 metre) requires surgical masks.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Infection Control & Epidemiology','medium','mcq','Clostridium difficile associated diarrhoea is a nosocomial infection. Why does alcohol hand sanitiser NOT adequately prevent C. difficile transmission?','["A. Alcohol is inactivated by the toxins produced by C. difficile in the environment","B. The concentration of alcohol in standard hand sanitisers is insufficient to kill vegetative C. difficile cells","C. C. difficile uses plasmid-encoded alcohol resistance genes","D. C. difficile forms spores that are resistant to alcohol; soap-and-water handwashing is required to mechanically remove spores"]','D','C. difficile forms highly resistant spores that are not killed by alcohol-based hand rubs. Spores are removed mechanically by friction and water with soap and water handwashing. Environmental decontamination requires sporicidal agents (sodium hypochlorite/bleach). C. difficile precautions also include contact isolation, and stopping the precipitating antibiotic (especially clindamycin, fluoroquinolones, cephalosporins).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Infection Control & Epidemiology','medium','mcq','S. epidermidis is the most common cause of prosthetic device and catheter-related bloodstream infections. Why is it difficult to treat?','["A. S. epidermidis produces ESBLs that render it resistant to all beta-lactams","B. S. epidermidis evades immune detection by molecular mimicry of host proteins","C. S. epidermidis is intrinsically resistant to vancomycin due to VanA genes","D. S. epidermidis forms biofilms on device surfaces, where organisms are metabolically quiescent and up to 1000-fold more resistant to antibiotics than planktonic forms"]','D','Biofilm on prosthetic material is the key virulence feature of S. epidermidis. Within biofilm, bacteria have reduced metabolic activity (stationary phase-like), impaired antibiotic penetration through the extracellular polysaccharide matrix, and altered gene expression. Antibiotic therapy alone is insufficient; device removal is usually required. Most healthcare isolates are MRSE (methicillin-resistant S. epidermidis).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Infection Control & Epidemiology','medium','mcq','Influenza and meningococcal meningitis require droplet precautions. What specific infection control measures are required for droplet precautions?','["A. Negative pressure isolation room and N95 respirators for all staff entering","B. Positive pressure isolation room and HEPA filtration of all room air","C. Full PPE including hazmat suit for all staff entering the room","D. Surgical mask when within 1 metre of the patient, private room or cohort nursing"]','D','Droplet precautions are required for pathogens transmitted by large respiratory droplets (>5 micrometres) generated by coughing, sneezing, or talking, which travel <1-2 metres and do not remain suspended. Requirements: (1) surgical/procedure mask when within 1 metre (not N95), (2) private room preferred or cohort with same infection. Examples: influenza, meningococcus, Group A Streptococcus, pertussis, mumps, rubella.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Infection Control & Epidemiology','medium','mcq','MRSA has become a major nosocomial pathogen. Which factors most commonly facilitate MRSA transmission in healthcare settings?','["A. Airborne spread from MRSA carriers to susceptible patients during ward rounds","B. Blood product transfusions carrying MRSA from asymptomatic donors","C. Shared food facilities and water supplies within hospital wards","D. Healthcare worker hand carriage (skin/nares) with transmission via direct contact or contaminated equipment"]','D','MRSA is transmitted primarily by contact — via transiently colonised healthcare worker hands and contaminated surfaces/equipment. S. aureus (including MRSA) is carried in the nasal passages of ~30% of staff. Prevention requires: hand hygiene, contact precautions (gloves and gown), screening and decolonisation (chlorhexidine washes, nasal mupirocin), and environmental cleaning.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Infection Control & Epidemiology','medium','mcq','The NAP1 hypervirulent strain of Clostridium difficile is associated with more severe disease outbreaks. What features make NAP1 (ribotype 027) more virulent?','["A. NAP1 is resistant to vancomycin, making first-line treatment ineffective","B. NAP1 produces a unique capsule preventing immune clearance and increasing transmissibility","C. NAP1 forms biofilms on colonic epithelium that prevent antibiotic penetration","D. NAP1 produces binary toxin (CDT) in addition to toxins A and B, produces more spores, and has a deletion mutation causing overproduction of toxins A and B"]','D','NAP1 (North American PFGE type 1)/ribotype 027 is hypervirulent due to: (1) deletion in tcdC regulatory gene causing overproduction of toxin A and B; (2) production of binary toxin (CDT/C. difficile transferase) which enhances mucosal adhesion; (3) increased spore formation (greater environmental persistence and transmission). Associated with higher rates of pseudomembranous colitis, toxic megacolon, colectomy, and death.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Infection Control & Epidemiology','hard','mcq','The distinction between sterilisation, high-level disinfection, and antisepsis is important in healthcare. Which correctly ranks these processes in order of decreasing microbicidal activity?','["A. Antisepsis > Disinfection > Sterilisation","B. Disinfection > Sterilisation > Antisepsis","C. Sterilisation > High-level disinfection > Antisepsis","D. All three processes achieve the same level of microbial killing"]','C','Hierarchy of microbicidal activity: (1) Sterilisation — kills ALL microorganisms including spores (autoclave, ethylene oxide, ionising radiation); (2) High-level disinfection — kills all organisms except high numbers of spores (glutaraldehyde, hydrogen peroxide — for semi-critical equipment like endoscopes); (3) Antisepsis — reduces microbial burden on living tissue to safe levels (chlorhexidine, povidone-iodine, alcohol for skin).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed'),
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Infectious Diseases','Anatomy & Physiology','Infection Control & Epidemiology','hard','mcq','Nosocomial urinary tract infections are the most common type of healthcare-associated infection. What is the most important risk factor?','["A. Female sex predisposing to ascending Gram-negative infection during hospitalisation","B. Previous antibiotic therapy disrupting normal urinary tract flora","C. Immunosuppression in post-transplant patients reducing mucosal defences","D. Presence and duration of an indwelling urinary catheter, with risk increasing ~5% per day of catheterisation"]','D','Urinary catheters are the most important risk factor for nosocomial UTI — catheter-associated UTI (CAUTI). Bacteria (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, Candida, Enterococcus) ascend along the catheter biofilm. Risk increases approximately 3-7% per day of catheterisation. The most effective prevention is avoiding unnecessary catheterisation and removing catheters as soon as possible. Biofilm forms on all catheter surfaces within hours of insertion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Clinical Microbiology Made Ridiculously Simple 6th Ed');
